  "evidence": "TEXT_D: civil liberties and social liberalisation (stronger Freedom of Information; no ID cards; judicial oversight of surveillance; Equality Act and Equality Commission; civil partnership registration); liberal criminal justice (end most mandatory sentencing; shift from prison expansion to effective alternatives; restorative and youth justice; independent police complaints); humanitarian asylum framing (fairer, faster asylum; end vouchers; improve support); pro‑EU/internationalism (EU constitutional settlement; enable euro entry by referendum; strengthen UN/ICC; oppose national missile defence; pursue disarmament); environmentalism (carbon tax; rising renewables targets; phase out nuclear; national recycling; GM moratorium; marine protected areas).\n\nTEXT_F: law‑and‑order/authority emphasis (mandatory prison for selling drugs to under‑16s; end automatic early release; tougher sentences near schools; require prison work with proceeds to victims; lifetime supervision for sex offenders; visible policing; easier exclusion for disruptive pupils, off‑site Progress Centres); restrictive asylum control (secure reception centres pending decisions; presumptive refusal from safe countries; removals agency); national sovereignty and traditionalism (keep the pound; retain UK veto; oppose separate EU military structures; support missile defence; maintain independent nuclear deterrent; English votes for English laws); family/traditional institutions (married couple’s allowance; transferable allowances for couples with young children; expansion of faith and parent‑run schools).",
  "reasoning": "TEXT_D argues from civil‑libertarian, equality‑focused, pro‑EU and environmental commitments characteristic of GAL, while TEXT_F argues from law‑and‑order, migration control, national sovereignty, and traditional family/authority commitments characteristic of TAN. Given the electorate’s slight TAN tilt (0.55 TAN vs 0.45 GAL), TEXT_F aligns more closely with the profile.",
